    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "A materia medica profile of lavender, its traditional actions, therapeutic uses across the nervous system, skin. And musculoskeletal systems, preparation methods, and safety. The most widely used aromatic herb in the European tradition, documented from Hildegard of Bingen to the EMA. A long kitchen tradition for everyday use at home. About 15 minutes of active work, keeping in a cool cupboard for several weeks.",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "attrs": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Traditional actions",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Lavender is a nerve-calming: it calms the nervous system. It is also a mild sedative and a gentle anti-anxiety herb. It eases muscle spasm in both smooth muscle and the larger muscles you can feel. Used on the skin, it calms swelling and acts as a mild antiseptic. It also warms the skin enough to ease tension in cold or cramped muscles.writer Hildegard of Bingen used lavender for 'migraine and a pure knowledge of God'. The first written European use as medicine. Old western herbals also list lavender as both a calming herb and a wound-cleaning herb on the skin. See the sources note for the older texts.",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "attrs": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Documented uses",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Internal use, as an steeped preparation: mild anxiety, restlessness, trouble sleeping, mild low mood, and tension headache. External use: insect bites and stings as a cool compress. Tight or sore muscles as a bath or compress; mild skin irritation as a compress or wash. Modern monographs confirm the traditional use for mild anxiety, restlessness. And sleep trouble, both as a tea and as a vapour. See the sources note for the modern reference.",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "attrs": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Evidence level",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Lavender has the strongest research record of any calming herb in the western tradition. A standardised lavender oil pill has been tested in several human trials. It is found to ease general anxiety and poor sleep at a much higher strength than a kitchen steeped preparation. The kitchen steeped preparation also has traditional status under modern monographs for mild anxiety and sleep trouble. The same biology that makes the pill work is at play in the cup of tea. Just at a lower strength.",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "attrs": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Preparations and doses",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "steeped preparation: 3, 5 g dried flowers per 250 ml water, steeped covered for 10 minutes. One to three cups daily. Bath: 30 g in 500 ml concentrated steeped preparation, added to warm bath. Compress: 5, 10 g in 200 ml water, cooled, applied to skin. alcohol-soaked preparation: 1:5 in 40% spirit. 2, 4 ml daily (less commonly made at home; commercial preparations are more consistent).",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "attrs": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Safety",
          "type": "text"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Lavender at normal steeped preparation and topical doses is safe for most adults. Including in pregnancy at culinary and low therapeutic concentrations. No much drug interactions are documented at steeped preparation doses. Lavender essential oil is not the same as the flower steeped preparation, the oil should not be ingested. For topical use on sensitive skin, test on a small area first. See your doctor for anxiety, low mood, or insomnia that is persistent or much impairing.",
          "type": "text",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
